      sound: "Blue Note’s difference began before the tape rolled. Unlike most independents, it paid musicians to rehearse before a session; as the producer Bob Porter put it, “The difference between Blue Note and Prestige is two days’ rehearsal” [2]. Van Gelder’s close, vivid recordings gave the label a recognizable sound, though not everyone loved it—Charles Mingus complained that Van Gelder “ruined my bass sound” [6]. From 1956 the designer Reid Miles, hired from Esquire, set Wolff’s session photographs against bold, asymmetric type, turning the twelve-inch sleeve into a piece of modernist graphic design [1][2].",
      significance: "Blue Note was a small business run by two German-Jewish émigrés recording mostly African American musicians, and its reputation for fairness is real but relative. Paid rehearsals and Lion’s appetite for original compositions set it apart from rivals [1][2], yet sidemen were still paid flat session fees, as across the industry [7], and the bassist Steve Swallow told JazzTimes in 2024: “It is a great blessing to jazz that Blue Note existed, but on the business side they were also sort of gangsters” [2]. Royalties became a live issue only when the catalog became a corporate asset. In 1992 EMI raised royalties to 10 percent for musicians who had recorded for its labels before 1970, and in 2001 a search of Blue Note’s books found more than $40,000 held in escrow for the pianist Jutta Hipp, who had recorded for the label in the 1950s [7].",
