      story: [
        "Lubinsky founded Savoy in Newark, New Jersey, in 1942 and built it on jazz, rhythm and blues and gospel [1]. Through producers such as Teddy Reig it recorded the young modernists—Charlie Parker, Dexter Gordon, Fats Navarro, J. J. Johnson, Erroll Garner, Miles Davis—while the major companies still kept their distance from bebop [1]. On November 26, 1945, at the WOR studios in New York, Parker’s group recorded “Billie’s Bounce,” “Now’s the Time” and “Ko Ko,” a blistering new line over the chords of “Cherokee” that became one of the founding documents of the style [2][3][4].",
        "From 1948 Savoy grew by buying smaller labels, and it became one of the most important companies in Black gospel [1]. Under Ozzie Cadena, from 1954 to 1962, it issued Cannonball Adderley’s first album as a leader and, in the early 1960s, briefly took up the avant-garde—Sun Ra, Paul Bley, Archie Shepp [1]. After Lubinsky’s death in 1974 the catalog was bought by Clive Davis’s Arista, then by Joe Fields of Muse; Nippon Columbia acquired it in 1991, and Concord in 2017 [1].",
      ],
      sound: "Savoy’s jazz sessions were run for speed and economy: a few hours in a rented studio, short 78-rpm takes and a premium on new tunes. Accounts of the “Ko Ko” date describe Reig steering Parker away from playing the “Cherokee” melody itself and toward originals, because Lubinsky would not pay for other people’s songs [3][4]. The result was a body of bebop “heads” written on borrowed chord changes—“Cherokee” became “Ko Ko”—a practice that suited the new style’s harmonic thinking and the label’s accounts at the same time [2].",
      significance: "Lubinsky’s reputation among musicians was poor. Many of Savoy’s Black artists felt underpaid, and the Newark journalist Tiny Price wrote that “there’s no doubt everybody hated Herman Lubinsky,” while conceding that much of their music would not otherwise have been recorded [1]. Savoy’s model was the independents’ model in its harshest form: a flat fee for the session, little or nothing in royalties, and a strong incentive to record “originals,” since a label that recorded a standard owed royalties to the song’s publisher [3][4]. The bebop canon was built, in part, on those terms.",
