        "Ross Russell, a writer who ran the Tempo Music Shop in Hollywood, started Dial in 1946, at first to press records for his own shop [1][2]. Dizzy Gillespie and Charlie Parker had brought bebop to Los Angeles with an engagement at Billy Berg’s club; when the rest of the band went home, Parker stayed [2]. On February 26, 1946 he signed an exclusive one-year contract with Russell—although, as one account notes, he was already under contract to Savoy [1]. A session on March 28 produced “Ornithology,” “Yardbird Suite,” “Moose the Mooche” and “A Night in Tunisia,” with Miles Davis on trumpet [1][2].",
