      lead: "Bob Weinstock’s Prestige recorded more of the 1950s’ great small groups than anyone—fast, cheaply and with almost no rehearsal—and so captured Miles Davis’s first great quintet, Sonny Rollins at his peak and the young John Coltrane.",
      story: [
        "Weinstock began as a teenager selling jazz records by mail, through advertisements in The Record Changer, from his family’s New York apartment [1][2]. In January 1949, aged twenty, he started a label called New Jazz, whose first release was by Lennie Tristano’s quintet, and later that year formed Prestige [2]. He could not read music or play an instrument and produced by ear [1]. In the early and middle 1950s Prestige recorded Stan Getz, Lee Konitz, Thelonious Monk, the Modern Jazz Quartet, Sonny Rollins, Gene Ammons and Miles Davis, much of it engineered by Rudy Van Gelder [3].",
        "The most famous Prestige dates were contractual. Davis had committed himself to Columbia but still owed Prestige several albums, and his quintet—John Coltrane, Red Garland, Paul Chambers and Philly Joe Jones—cleared the debt in two marathon sessions at Van Gelder’s Hackensack studio on May 11 and October 26, 1956 [4]. Prestige spread the results over four albums, Cookin’, Relaxin’, Workin’ and Steamin’, released between 1957 and 1961, so that new Davis records kept appearing long after he had left [4].",
        "From the late 1950s Weinstock left the sessions to staff producers, and organ-led soul jazz—Jack McDuff, Shirley Scott, Charles Earland—helped keep the company viable, alongside subsidiaries for mainstream swing, ballads and the blues revival [2][3]. Weinstock sold Prestige to Fantasy in 1971 and moved to Florida, where he died in 2006 [1][3]. Under Fantasy and then Concord, the catalog became one of the backbones of the reissue business, and Concord revived the name for new recordings in 2017 [3].",
      ],
      sound: "Prestige brought the jam session into the studio. Musicians were booked for a day, recorded with “almost no rehearsal time,” and fell back on a blues if the material ran short [1][5]. Weinstock preferred one take per tune, arguing that further takes lost spontaneity [2]. Critics have long blamed thrift. Tad Richards, who chronicled the label’s sessions, writes that there is “some truth to that” but that it is not the whole story: the method caught working bands as they actually sounded on the bandstand [5][6].",
      significance: "Prestige was where young players got their first albums—Rollins’s first sessions as a leader, Coltrane’s first album under his own name—and where many of them left as soon as they could: Monk to Riverside, Davis to Columbia, Coltrane to Atlantic [3][4]. Like most independents it paid by the session; royalties, where they existed at all, went to leaders at low rates [7]. That model made jazz cheap to record in quantity, and it left the long-term value of the catalog—reissued for decades by Fantasy and Concord—with the company rather than with the musicians.",
