      lead: "Best known for rhythm and blues and soul, Atlantic also ran, under Nesuhi Ertegun, the most adventurous jazz list of the late 1950s: the Modern Jazz Quartet, Charles Mingus, John Coltrane’s Giant Steps and Ornette Coleman’s The Shape of Jazz to Come.",
      story: [
        "Ahmet Ertegun, son of Turkey’s ambassador in Washington, founded Atlantic in New York in October 1947 with Herb Abramson, after persuading the family dentist, Vahdi Sabit, to invest $10,000 [1]. Its first records, issued in January 1948, leaned toward modern jazz, but the company’s fortune came from rhythm and blues and, after Jerry Wexler joined in 1953, from the music that became soul [1]. Early jazz artists on the label included Sidney Bechet, Dizzy Gillespie, Erroll Garner, Mary Lou Williams and Earl Hines, and in 1948 the American Federation of Musicians’ recording ban forced it to stockpile recordings [1].",
        "In January 1955 Nesuhi Ertegun, Ahmet’s older brother, joined as a partner in charge of jazz and LPs [1][2]. He had helped run the Jazz Man record shop and label in Los Angeles and worked for Lester Koenig at Good Time Jazz, and he brought a collector’s ear and West Coast contacts [2]. He reserved a 1200 series for jazz LPs and recorded the Modern Jazz Quartet, Mingus, Coltrane after his Prestige years and Coleman after his two Contemporary albums [1][2]. By 1958 Atlantic was described as America’s second-largest independent jazz label [1].",
        "In May 1959 the two ends of the list met history in the same month: Coltrane recorded most of Giant Steps at Atlantic’s New York studio on May 4–5, and Coleman’s quartet recorded The Shape of Jazz to Come at Radio Recorders in Hollywood on May 22 [3][4]. Atlantic later issued Coleman’s Free Jazz, Coltrane’s My Favorite Things and records by Rahsaan Roland Kirk and Charles Lloyd. In 1967 the company was sold to Warner Bros.–Seven Arts for $17.5 million [1]. A 1978 warehouse fire destroyed or damaged thousands of tapes, including most of Atlantic’s unreleased masters from 1948 to 1969 [1].",
      ],
      sound: "Atlantic was technically ahead of most rivals. Tom Dowd, who as a young draftee had worked on the Manhattan Project at Columbia University, ran its sessions; he recorded in stereo early, running a portable stereo machine alongside the studio’s mono deck, and persuaded the partners to install an Ampex eight-track recorder, making Atlantic the first record company to work in multitrack [1][5]. Nesuhi, for his part, invested in the LP as an object: better recording, better pressings, better sleeves [2].",
      significance: "Atlantic was an immigrant family’s business that made its money selling Black music, first to Black and then to white audiences. In jazz, Nesuhi’s list showed that an independent with pop hits could underwrite artistic risk: a record like Coleman’s Free Jazz, a double-quartet collective improvisation filling an entire LP, was hardly a commercial proposition. When Atlantic was sold to Warner, its jazz catalog went with it into a corporate conglomerate, and the 1978 fire showed what could be lost when such archives were treated as warehouse stock [1].",
