      story: [
        "In 1960 ABC-Paramount hired Creed Taylor to start a jazz line; the intended name, Pulse, was already taken, so it became Impulse! [1]. Taylor’s first releases set the pattern—Ray Charles’s Genius + Soul = Jazz (the first hit), Gil Evans’s Out of the Cool, Oliver Nelson’s The Blues and the Abstract Truth—and he signed John Coltrane before leaving for Verve in 1961 [1][3].",
        "Bob Thiele, who ran the label from 1961 to 1969, produced most of what followed, beginning with Coltrane’s Live! at the Village Vanguard [1][6]. He recorded the classic quartet with McCoy Tyner, Jimmy Garrison and Elvin Jones, Coltrane’s meetings with Duke Ellington and Johnny Hartman and, on December 9, 1964, at Van Gelder’s Englewood Cliffs studio, A Love Supreme, which had sold about 500,000 copies by 1970, against Coltrane’s usual 30,000 [4]. Coltrane, in turn, urged the label to record younger free players—Archie Shepp, Pharoah Sanders, Albert Ayler—so that Impulse!, nicknamed “the house that Trane built,” became a key outlet for the new music [1][2]. Mingus’s The Black Saint and the Sinner Lady and Charlie Haden’s Liberation Music Orchestra were Impulse! records too.",
        "After Coltrane’s death in 1967, the label carried his circle into the 1970s under Ed Michel: Alice Coltrane’s Journey in Satchidananda, Sanders’s Karma, Keith Jarrett’s American quartet and reissues of Sun Ra’s Saturn albums [1][5]. New recording stopped in the late 1970s, and MCA bought the company in 1979 [1]. Revived in 1992 under MCA’s GRP, Impulse! has since become part of Universal’s Verve Label Group, which has used the name for new recordings as well as for the reissues that keep its 1960s catalog in print [1].",
      ],
      sound: "Impulse! sold an aesthetic. Fran Scott (later Fran Attaway) devised the black, orange and white livery; most LPs came in glossy gatefold sleeves, often with photographs by Pete Turner, Arnold Newman or Chuck Stewart, and the back covers promised “The New Wave of Jazz is on IMPULSE!” [1][3]. Many of the label’s New York sessions were engineered by Rudy Van Gelder, giving it a sound kin to Blue Note’s and Prestige’s [2].",
      significance: "Impulse! shows that a corporate parent could, for a time, underwrite radical art. ABC-Paramount spent heavily on packaging and promotion, and the label sold music about Black spirituality and freedom—Coltrane’s “Alabama,” Shepp’s Fire Music, Sanders’s Karma—to a mass audience [2]. The arrangement rested on Coltrane’s sales and prestige; after his death, and as jazz sales fell, the parent company’s commitment faded [2].",
