        "Bernard Stollman, a New York lawyer who had begun his career working on the estates of Charlie Parker and Billie Holiday, founded ESP-Disk’ to issue a record of songs in Esperanto, the international language [1][2]. Its second release changed its purpose: Albert Ayler’s Spiritual Unity, a trio with Gary Peacock and Sunny Murray recorded in 1964, whose raw, cry-like sound became a manifesto of the new music [1][3]. With money from his parents, Stollman followed it with Pharoah Sanders’s first album, the New York Art Quartet, The Heliocentric Worlds of Sun Ra, Paul Bley, Giuseppi Logan and Ornette Coleman’s 1962 Town Hall concert [2][3].",
        "ESP also released underground rock and spoken word—the Fugs, the Godz, Pearls Before Swine, William Burroughs, Timothy Leary [2]. The money ran out in the early 1970s, and Stollman closed the label in 1974 and went back to law, later serving as an assistant New York attorney general [2]. He reactivated ESP in 2005 and died in 2015 [2][4]. His later years were not free of controversy: Irwin Chusid, who administers Sun Ra’s estate, accused him of collecting royalties on Sun Ra recordings without authority [2].",
