        "After producing at Bethlehem, starting Impulse! and running Verve, Creed Taylor incorporated Creed Taylor, Inc. in 1966; from 1967 it produced records for A&M under the CTI name, beginning with Wes Montgomery’s A Day in the Life [1][2]. In 1970 CTI became independent of A&M [2]. Its catalog set hard-bop and post-bop soloists in sumptuous settings—Freddie Hubbard’s Red Clay, Stanley Turrentine’s Sugar, George Benson, Hubert Laws, Milt Jackson, Ron Carter—and in 1971 Taylor added Kudu, a soul-jazz sister label that made Grover Washington Jr. a star [2].",
